### What does a hand-written producer/consumer look like?

With a hand-written integration you own the entire lifecycle: configure a client, build records, call send, handle futures, deal with retries, write the poll loop, deserialize, process, commit offsets, handle rebalances, manage threading and shutdown.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class CustomProducerExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringSerializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.RETRIES_CONFIG, 3);

        try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props)) {
            // You handle: record construction, error handling, retries, monitoring
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("settlements", "SAV2001",
                "{\"amount\":50000}")).get();
            System.out.println("Custom producer: you own every line of plumbing");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Custom producer: you own every line of plumbing
```

### What does Kafka Connect look like for the same job?

Connect removes the plumbing. You submit a JSON configuration and the runtime runs it.

```json
{
  "name": "settlements-jdbc-source",
  "config": {
    "connector.class": "io.confluent.connect.jdbc.JdbcSourceConnector",
    "connection.url": "jdbc:postgresql://db-1:5432/banking",
    "connection.user": "kafka_connect",
    "table.whitelist": "settlements",
    "mode": "timestamp+incrementing",
    "timestamp.column.name": "updated_at",
    "incrementing.column.name": "id",
    "topic.prefix": "db.",
    "tasks.max": "3"
  }
}
```

```bash
curl -X POST http://connect-1:8083/connectors \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d @config.json
# No code. Runtime handles scaling, restarts, offset tracking.
```

### What does Connect handle that you must build yourself?

| Concern | Kafka Connect | Custom Code |
|---|---|---|
| Scaling | Automatic (tasks across workers) | You build thread pools |
| Fault tolerance | Restart failed tasks on healthy workers | You handle restarts |
| Offset tracking | Automatic (source and consumer offsets) | You commit manually |
| Configuration | JSON config, REST API | Java properties, code |
| Monitoring | REST API status endpoint | Custom metrics |
| Connector ecosystem | 100+ pre-built | You write from scratch |

```bash
# Connect: check all connectors and their status
curl http://connect-1:8083/connectors | jq
curl http://connect-1:8083/connectors/settlements-jdbc-source/status | jq
```

### When do you choose Kafka Connect?

Choose Connect when a suitable connector exists, the transformation is light (use SMTs), and you want operational concerns handled for you. This is the majority of data-in, data-out cases.

```bash
# Common connectors available:
# JDBC Source/Sink      -- any relational database
# S3 Sink               -- archive to object storage
# Elasticsearch Sink    -- full-text search index
# MongoDB Source/Sink   -- document store
# Debezium CDC Source   -- change data capture from DB logs
# Google BigQuery Sink  -- data warehouse loading
```

### When do you choose a custom producer/consumer?

Choose custom code when no connector fits the system or protocol, when you need rich stateful business logic, when the processing is really an application, or when you want tight control over semantics.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.*;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;

public class CustomFraudScorer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "fraud-scorer");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class.getName());

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(List.of("card.authorizations"));
            while (true) {
                ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
                for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
                    // Rich business logic: score fraud, branch on result
                    double score = scoreFraud(record.value());
                    if (score > 0.8) blockCard(record.key());
                    // No connector can express this per-record decision
                }
            }
        }
    }

    static double scoreFraud(String txn) { return 0.85; }
    static void blockCard(String accountId) {
        System.out.println("BLOCK CARD: " + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
BLOCK CARD: SAV2001
```

### What is the common anti-pattern?

Reinventing a JDBC source as a brittle custom poller when a connector already exists. The custom poller loses its place on restart, misses deletes, and adds query load to the database.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class AntiPatternDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: custom poller that does what JDBC Source Connector already does
        System.out.println("WRONG: SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE updated_at > last_poll");
        System.out.println("Problems: misses deletes, loses offset on restart, adds DB load");

        // CORRECT: use the JDBC Source Connector
        System.out.println("CORRECT: Submit JDBC Source config to Connect runtime");
        System.out.println("Handles: offset tracking, restart, scaling, fault tolerance");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE updated_at > last_poll
Problems: misses deletes, loses offset on restart, adds DB load
CORRECT: Submit JDBC Source config to Connect runtime
Handles: offset tracking, restart, scaling, fault tolerance
```

### What about heavy processing?

Heavy enrichment, joins, or stateful processing belongs in a stream-processing application (Kafka Streams, Flink), with Connect handling ingest and egress at the edges.

```text
# Architecture:
# Connect Source -> Kafka topic -> Stream processing app -> Kafka topic -> Connect Sink
#   (data in)                        (business logic)                     (data out)

# Connect handles the edges; stream processing handles the middle
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Connect runs in distributed mode in production for resilience. Deploy it alongside custom apps. Connect is at-least-once by default, so sink idempotency still matters. Use Avro with Schema Registry for production-grade schema evolution.

```bash
# Decision tree:
# Is there a connector for this system? -> Yes -> Use Connect
#                                        -> No  -> Write custom code
# Does the data need stateful processing? -> Yes -> Stream processing app
#                                           -> No  -> Connect with SMTs
```
